/E (8. What are aggregate function in SQL?)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
283 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Aggregate functions in SQL are used to perform calculations on groups of rows to return a single result. Common aggregate functions include SUM\(\), AVG\(\), MAX\(\), MIN\(\), and COUNT\(\). They help summarize data by performing operations like adding up values, finding averages, or identifying the highest or lowest values within a dataset.)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
284 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (9. What are constraints in SQL?)
/E (9. What are constraints in SQL?)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
285 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Constraints in SQL are rules or conditions applied to a table column to ensure data integrity and accuracy. They enforce limits or restrictions on the type, format, or range of values that can be stored in a column. Examples include NOT NULL \(requiring a column to have a value\), UNIQUE \(ensuring each value in a column is unique\), PRIMARY KEY \(defining a unique identifier for each row\), FOREIGN KEY \(establishing a relationship between tables\), and CHECK \(specifying conditions that must be met for data to be valid\).)
/Pg 9 0 R
/K [12]
>>
endobj
