295 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (10. What are keys in SQL and why do we use them and its types?)
/E (10. What are keys in SQL and why do we use them and its types?)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
296 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Keys in SQL are columns or combinations of columns that are used to uniquely identify each row in a table. They ensure data integrity and provide a way to establish relationships between tables. There are several types of keys:)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
297 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 885 0 R
/ActualText (Primary Key: A primary key uniquely identifies each row in a table. It must contain unique values and cannot contain NULL values.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
298 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 886 0 R
/ActualText (Foreign Key: A foreign key is a column or a set of columns in one table that refers to the primary key in another table. It establishes a link between two tables and enforces referential integrity.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
299 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 887 0 R
/ActualText (Unique Key: A unique key ensures that all values in a column \(or a combination of columns\) are unique. Unlike a primary key, it can contain NULL values.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
300 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 888 0 R
/ActualText (Candidate Key: A candidate key is a column or a set of columns that can uniquely identify a row in a table. It can be chosen as the primary key.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
301 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (These keys help maintain data consistency, enforce relationships between tables, and optimize database performance.)
/Pg 10 0 R
/K [9]
>>
