<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (28. Explain database white box testing and black box testing?)
/E (28. Explain database white box testing and black box testing?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
413 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 913 0 R
/ActualText (Database white box testing involves examining the internal structures and code of a database system to ensure its correctness, efficiency, and security. This type of testing focuses on the logic, algorithms, and database schema to uncover any bugs or issues within the database implementation.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
414 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 914 0 R
/ActualText (Black box testing, on the other hand, treats the database system as a closed entity, where the tester interacts with the system through its inputs and observes the outputs. It doesn't require knowledge of the internal workings of the database. The goal of black box testing is to validate the functionality and behavior of the database system according to its specifications, without considering its internal implementation details.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
415 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/T (29. What is the difference between the DROP and TRUNCATE commands?)
/E (29. What is the difference between the DROP and TRUNCATE commands?)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
416 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText ( DROP command is used to completely remove a table from a database along with all its data and structure, whereas TRUNCATE command is used to remove all rows from a table, but it keeps the table structure intact.)
/Pg 16 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
