/T (35. What are stored procedures, and how are they different from functions?)
/E (35. What are stored procedures, and how are they different from functions?)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
446 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (Stored procedures are precompiled SQL code that is stored in a database and can be executed on demand. They are different from functions in that stored procedures can perform a series of SQL statements and other procedural logic, whereas functions typically return a single value and are used within SQL statements.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
447 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (36. Describe the ACID properties in the context of database transactions.)
/E (36. Describe the ACID properties in the context of database transactions.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
448 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/ActualText (ACID stands for Atomicity, Consistency, Isolation, and Durability. It's a set of properties that ensure reliability and consistency in database transactions:)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
449 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 915 0 R
/ActualText (Atomicity ensures that either all operations in a transaction are completed successfully, or none are.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
450 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 916 0 R
/ActualText (Consistency guarantees that the database remains in a valid state before and after the transaction.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
451 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 917 0 R
/ActualText (Isolation ensures that the operations within a transaction are isolated from other concurrent transactions until the transaction is completed.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
452 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 918 0 R
/ActualText (Durability ensures that once a transaction is committed, its effects are permanent and survive system failures.)
/Pg 18 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
453 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/T (37. Explain the use of the HAVING clause in SQL.)
